# AI SDK with MCP

Use the [AI SDK](https://vercel.com/docs/ai-sdk) to call tools from a [Model Context Protocol (MCP)](https://vercel.com/docs/mcp) server hosted on Vercel. The `@ai-sdk/mcp` client discovers the server's tools, and [AI Gateway](https://vercel.com/docs/ai-gateway) handles model requests.

Before you begin, you need:

- A JavaScript or TypeScript project with Node.js 22 or later. This example uses [AI SDK 7](https://vercel.com/docs/ai-gateway/sdks-and-apis/ai-sdk#version-compatibility) and ECMAScript modules (ESM).
- An [AI Gateway API key](https://vercel.com/docs/ai-gateway/authentication-and-byok/api-keys).
- The `roll_dice` MCP server from [Deploy MCP servers to Vercel](https://vercel.com/docs/mcp/deploy-mcp-servers-to-vercel#deploy-an-mcp-server-on-vercel), deployed at a URL your client can reach.

Install the AI SDK and its MCP client:

Save your AI Gateway API key and deployed MCP endpoint in a `.env` file. Replace `your-project` with your deployment's domain:

.env

```
AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=your_api_key_here
MCP_SERVER_URL=https://your-project.vercel.app/api/mcp
```

If your MCP server requires a bearer token, also set `MCP_SERVER_TOKEN` to a token the server accepts. For OAuth consent and token refresh, follow [Vercel Connect with AI SDK and MCP](https://vercel.com/docs/connect/frameworks/ai-sdk-and-mcp).

For a deployment with [Deployment Protection](https://vercel.com/docs/deployment-protection), also configure [automation access](https://vercel.com/docs/deployment-protection/methods-to-bypass-deployment-protection/protection-bypass-automation) on the MCP client transport.

Create an entry file that connects over Streamable HTTP and passes the discovered tools to `generateText`. Run this code on the server or from your terminal to keep credentials private:

index.mts

```
import { createMCPClient } from '@ai-sdk/mcp';
import { generateText, isStepCount } from 'ai';

const url = process.env.MCP_SERVER_URL;
if (!url) {
  throw new Error('Set MCP_SERVER_URL in your .env file.');
}

const token = process.env.MCP_SERVER_TOKEN;
const client = await createMCPClient({
  transport: {
    type: 'http',
    url,
    ...(token ? { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } } : {}),
  },
});

try {
  const tools = await client.tools();
  const result = await generateText({
    model: 'anthropic/claude-opus-5',
    tools,
    prompt: 'Use roll_dice to roll a six-sided die once.',
    stopWhen: isStepCount(5),
  });

  console.log(result.text);
} finally {
  await client.close();
}
```

The stop condition allows up to five model steps so the model can call the tool and respond with its result. The `finally` block closes the MCP client after generation succeeds or fails.

Run the TypeScript example with `tsx`, loading credentials from `.env`:

For JavaScript, run `node --env-file=.env index.mjs`. Your terminal displays the model's response with a dice result between one and six.

No. On Vercel, the AI SDK authenticates to AI Gateway with your project's OIDC token, so deployed code doesn't need `AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY`. A non-empty API key takes priority over OIDC, even if the key is invalid, so remove the key from your project's environment variables to use OIDC. For local development, run `vercel link` and `vercel env pull` to write `VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN` to `.env.local`. Your MCP server token is a separate credential that you still pass in the transport's `headers`. See [AI Gateway OIDC authentication](https://vercel.com/docs/ai-gateway/authentication-and-byok/oidc).

Pass `schemas` to `client.tools()`. The client then loads only the tools you list and types their inputs with your Zod schemas. Add an `outputSchema` to validate a tool's `structuredContent` and get a typed result. Without `schemas`, `client.tools()` loads every tool the server offers and infers input types from the server's schemas at runtime, so your code has no compile-time type checking. See [Using MCP tools](https://ai-sdk.dev/docs/ai-sdk-core/mcp-tools#using-mcp-tools) in the AI SDK documentation.

Yes. The AI SDK copies each MCP tool's annotations, such as `readOnlyHint` and `destructiveHint`, to the tool call's `toolMetadata.annotations`. Read them in the `toolApproval` option and request user approval for any tool the server doesn't mark as read-only. The client doesn't create an approval policy from annotations automatically. Annotations are hints from the server, so combine them with a tool allowlist and narrowly scoped credentials. See [Tool annotations and approval](https://ai-sdk.dev/docs/ai-sdk-core/mcp-tools#tool-annotations-and-approval).

Yes, during local development. Import `StdioMCPTransport` from `@ai-sdk/mcp/mcp-stdio` and give it the command that starts the server. A stdio server runs as a subprocess of your application, so it can't serve a production deployment. In production, connect over the HTTP transport, as this guide does. The client also supports SSE for servers that still use it.

Set `maxRetries` on `createMCPClient` to retry `tools/call` requests after transient HTTP or network failures. Retries are off by default, so turn them on only for tools that are safe to repeat. For agents that run for a long time or wait for a person, run the agent loop with `WorkflowAgent` from `@ai-sdk/workflow` on [Vercel Workflows](https://vercel.com/docs/workflows). When you mark a tool's `execute` function with `'use step'`, each call becomes a durable step that retries up to three times by default and survives restarts. Workflow steps pass only serializable values, so create and close the MCP client inside the step that calls the tool. See [What is WorkflowAgent?](https://vercel.com/kb/guide/what-is-workflowagent).

- Follow [Vercel Connect with AI SDK and MCP](https://vercel.com/docs/connect/frameworks/ai-sdk-and-mcp) for a streaming route with user consent and OAuth authentication.
- Configure model requests with [AI SDK and AI Gateway](https://vercel.com/docs/ai-gateway/sdks-and-apis/ai-sdk).
- Explore [MCP integrations](https://vercel.com/docs/mcp/integrations) for TanStack AI and eve examples.

